# Fingerprints on relations

A reference is what an author writes to point at a section: an anchor in code, which composes the section, or an anchor in prose, which links to it. The author who wrote the reference read the section as it stood on the day. When the section changes later, nothing tells the author that the reference may no longer hold.

A relation is the edge Loom derives from a reference, from the place where the reference stands to the section it points to. This chapter gives each relation a fingerprint. Loom keeps what a section looked like when the author last approved a reference to it, and it reports the reference as drifted once the section no longer matches. So no code and no prose drifts in silence from a section it depends on.

## A relation

A `Relation` says where a reference stands and what it points to. Its `loom` and its `section` hold the reference, its `kind` is the kind of reference, and `to` is the name the reference writes. It also says where the reference stands, as `at`, and which section the reference points to, as `target`.

A relation has a status, which Loom works out each time and never stores:

- **`untracked`:** nobody has approved the reference, so Loom keeps no fingerprint for it.
- **`inStep`:** the section still matches the fingerprint Loom keeps.
- **`drifted`:** the section no longer matches it.

A `KeptRelation` is what the workspace keeps of a relation an author approved. It follows the rule of the chapter "Kept by content, labelled by name". Its `fingerprint` is the identity: the fingerprint of the section as the author approved it. Its `loom`, its `section`, and its `to` are labels, the names the relation went by when Loom last wrote it. Its `kind` is kept beside them. Nothing else of a relation is kept.

=>

export const KeptRelationSchema = Schema.Struct({
  loom: Schema.String,
  section: Schema.String,
  kind: Schema.String,
  to: Schema.String,
  fingerprint: Schema.String,
})
export type KeptRelation = typeof KeptRelationSchema.Type

export type RelationStatus = 'untracked' | 'inStep' | 'drifted'

export type Relation = {
  readonly loom: Location
  readonly section: string
  readonly kind: string
  readonly to: string
  readonly at: Position
  readonly target: SectionRef
  readonly status: RelationStatus
}

## The references that make relations

Three sorts of reference make a relation. An anchor in code composes a section or names its module, and its relation is of the kind `anchor`. An anchor in prose links to a section, and its relation is of the kind `link`. A parameter that holds a reference names a section in a pair of braces, as `replaces=Postgres` does on a heading, and its relation takes the parameter's key for its kind, `replaces` here. Loom reads no meaning into the key. It follows the relation, and the trope that declared the key knows what the relation says.

Two things that point somewhere make no relation. A box that points to a chapter already copies a summary of what it points to and goes stale with it, as the chapter "Boxes" describes. The `anchors` value of a trope box says where the trope lives, and the anchors that name the trope make the relations to it.

A reference makes a relation only when it points into another loom. Inside one loom the prose and the code of every section sit together under the author's eyes, and a section that assembles a file refers to every section it composes. A fingerprint there would mark those references at every edit.

A `ReferenceFinder` finds one sort of reference in a loom. For each it gives the section that holds it, its kind, the name it writes, where it stands, and the section it points to. `referenceFinders` lists the three, and a further sort of reference joins by adding a finder.

=>

type Found = Pick<Relation, 'section' | 'kind' | 'to' | 'at' | 'target'>

type ReferenceFinder = (catalogue: Catalogue, module: LoomModule) => ReadonlyArray<Found>

const inAnotherLoom =
  (module: LoomModule) =>
  (target: SectionRef): boolean =>
    target.module !== module.path

~

An anchor in code reaches the product as an edge. An edge the loom bound to one of its own sections stays inside the loom, so `anchorsIn` passes it by. Any other edge resolves through the book, as the chapter "The product pass" describes, and `openEdge` keeps it when it resolves to one section of another loom. `foundInPart` gives the references one part of a section's code makes.

=>

type Edge = {
  readonly name: string
  readonly target: Option.Option<unknown>
  readonly anchor: Position
}

const openEdge =
  (catalogue: Catalogue, module: LoomModule, section: string) =>
  (edge: Edge): ReadonlyArray<Found> =>
    Option.isSome(edge.target)
      ? []
      : pipe(
          resolveName(catalogue, module.path, edge.name),
          Option.filter(inAnotherLoom(module)),
          Option.map(
            (target): Found => ({
              section,
              kind: 'anchor',
              to: edge.name,
              at: edge.anchor,
              target,
            }),
          ),
          Option.toArray,
        )

const foundInPart = byType<
  Part,
  [
    within: {
      readonly catalogue: Catalogue
      readonly module: LoomModule
      readonly host: Code
    },
  ]
>()<ReadonlyArray<Found>>({
  NameRef: (matched, { catalogue, module, host }) =>
    openEdge(catalogue, module, host.origin.name)(matched),
  ModuleRef: (matched, { catalogue, module, host }) =>
    openEdge(catalogue, module, host.origin.name)(matched),
  Fragment: (): ReadonlyArray<Found> => [],
})

const anchorsIn: ReferenceFinder = (catalogue, module) =>
  module.product.code.flatMap((host) =>
    host.fragments.flatMap((part) => foundInPart(part, { catalogue, module, host })),
  )

~

A link stands in the prose of a section. `linksIn` resolves each link of each section and keeps the ones that point into another loom.

=>

const linksIn: ReferenceFinder = (catalogue, module) =>
  module.doc.sections.flatMap((section) =>
    sectionLinksOf(section).flatMap((link) =>
      pipe(
        resolveAnchor(catalogue, module.path, link),
        Option.filter(inAnotherLoom(module)),
        Option.map(
          (target): Found => ({
            section: sectionNameOf(section),
            kind: 'link',
            to: link.name.value,
            at: link.name.position,
            target,
          }),
        ),
        Option.toArray,
      ),
    ),
  )

~

A reference that braces hold stands on a heading, an anchor, or a box, and the corpus reads every one a loom holds, as the chapter "The multi-file AST" describes. `parametersIn` keeps the ones that stand in a section and name one section of another loom. It passes by a trope box's `anchors` value.

=>

const parametersIn: ReferenceFinder = (catalogue, module) =>
  referencesOf(module).flatMap((held) =>
    pipe(
      held.section,
      Option.filter(() => !isTropeEntry(held)),
      Option.flatMap((section) =>
        pipe(
          resolveReference(catalogue, module.path, held),
          Option.filter(inAnotherLoom(module)),
          Option.map(
            (target): Found => ({
              section: sectionNameOf(section),
              kind: held.key,
              to: held.value.value,
              at: held.value.position,
              target,
            }),
          ),
        ),
      ),
      Option.toArray,
    ),
  )

export const referenceFinders: ReadonlyArray<ReferenceFinder> = [anchorsIn, linksIn, parametersIn]

## A fingerprint says whether, never where

The fingerprint of a section is a hash of what the section says, its prose and its code together. The heading is left out, so a section that was renamed keeps its fingerprint. Blank lines and spaces at the end of a line do not count either, so a section that was only tidied keeps it too. `said` gathers the text under the heading, and `fingerprintOf` hashes what `settled` makes of that text.

A fingerprint answers one question: is this still the text the author approved. It never finds a place in a loom. Tokens say where a reference stands.

=>

const settled = (text: string): string =>
  text
    .split('\n')
    .map((line) => line.trimEnd())
    .filter((line) => line !== '')
    .join('\n')

const said = (section: LoomSection): string =>
  [...section.preamble, ...section.code].map((weft) => weft.source).join('')

export const fingerprintOf = (section: LoomSection): string =>
  createHash('sha256').update(settled(said(section))).digest('hex')

## The relations of a loom

`relationsOf` derives the relations of a loom: one for each reference the finders find, with the status that the kept relations give it. A `Seen` is one such reference as it stands now, with its labels and the fingerprint its section has `now`.

A kept relation finds its reference by the labels first. `keyOf` joins the labels, and a reference that answers to them is the kept relation's reference. Its status is `inStep` when the section still has the kept fingerprint, and `drifted` when it does not.

A reference that answers to no labels may still have been approved under other names. So Loom looks at the strays. A stray is a kept relation that no reference answers to: one labelled with this loom, or one whose loom is no longer among the looms of the book. A stray follows a reference of its own kind whose section still has the kept fingerprint. That is the same text under a new name: a section that was renamed, a section that moved, or a loom that moved. A stray labelled with another loom follows only a reference with its own section and name, since a loom that moved kept both.

One stray follows one reference. `followerOf` picks the one that shares the most labels with it, so an approval never spreads to a second reference that nobody approved. A `Tracked` pairs a reference with the kept relation it found, by labels or by content, and `trackedIn` gives the pairs of a loom.

=>

type Named = Pick<Relation, 'loom' | 'section' | 'kind' | 'to'>

type Seen = Named & Pick<Relation, 'at' | 'target'> & { readonly now: string }

type Tracked = { readonly seen: Seen; readonly kept: Option.Option<KeptRelation> }

const keyOf = (named: Named): string =>
  JSON.stringify([named.loom, named.section, named.kind, named.to])

const seenIn = (catalogue: Catalogue, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Seen> =>
  referenceFinders.flatMap((find) =>
    find(catalogue, module).map((found): Seen => ({
      loom: module.path,
      section: found.section,
      kind: found.kind,
      to: found.to,
      at: found.at,
      target: found.target,
      now: fingerprintOf(found.target.section),
    })),
  )

const sharedLabels = (held: KeptRelation, seen: Seen): number =>
  (held.section === seen.section ? 1 : 0) + (held.to === seen.to ? 1 : 0)

const follows =
  (module: LoomModule, held: KeptRelation) =>
  (seen: Seen): boolean =>
    held.kind === seen.kind &&
    held.fingerprint === seen.now &&
    (held.loom === module.path || sharedLabels(held, seen) === 2)

const followerOf =
  (module: LoomModule, unanswered: ReadonlyArray<Seen>) =>
  (held: KeptRelation): Option.Option<Seen> =>
    unanswered.filter(follows(module, held)).reduce(
      (best, seen) =>
        Option.match(best, {
          onNone: () => Option.some(seen),
          onSome: (chosen) =>
            sharedLabels(held, seen) > sharedLabels(held, chosen) ? Option.some(seen) : best,
        }),
      Option.none<Seen>(),
    )

const trackedIn = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  module: LoomModule,
  kept: ReadonlyArray<KeptRelation>,
): ReadonlyArray<Tracked> => {
  const seen = seenIn(catalogue, module)
  const labelled = new Map(kept.map((held) => [keyOf(held), held] as const))
  const answering = (one: Seen): Option.Option<KeptRelation> =>
    Option.fromNullishOr(labelled.get(keyOf(one)))
  const answered = new Set(seen.map(keyOf))
  const strays = kept.filter((held) =>
    held.loom === module.path ? !answered.has(keyOf(held)) : !catalogue.modules.has(held.loom),
  )
  const follower = followerOf(
    module,
    seen.filter((one) => Option.isNone(answering(one))),
  )
  const followed = new Map(
    pipe(
      strays.map((held) => Option.map(follower(held), (one) => [keyOf(one), held] as const)),
      Array.getSomes,
      Array.reverse,
    ),
  )
  return seen.map((one) => ({
    seen: one,
    kept: Option.orElse(answering(one), () => Option.fromNullishOr(followed.get(keyOf(one)))),
  }))
}

const statusOf = (tracked: Tracked): RelationStatus =>
  Option.match(tracked.kept, {
    onNone: () => 'untracked',
    onSome: (held) => (held.fingerprint === tracked.seen.now ? 'inStep' : 'drifted'),
  })

const relationOf = (tracked: Tracked): Relation => ({
  loom: tracked.seen.loom,
  section: tracked.seen.section,
  kind: tracked.seen.kind,
  to: tracked.seen.to,
  at: tracked.seen.at,
  target: tracked.seen.target,
  status: statusOf(tracked),
})

export const relationsOf = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  module: LoomModule,
  kept: ReadonlyArray<KeptRelation>,
): ReadonlyArray<Relation> => trackedIn(catalogue, module, kept).map(relationOf)

## Drift

`driftFaults` gives each drifted relation of a loom the `DriftedReference` fault, on the reference itself. The fault is a warning. A tangle goes ahead all the same, since a drifted reference still composes.

An untracked relation carries nothing. So a book that starts to use fingerprints does not light up, and an author takes them up one reference at a time.

Loom keeps a fingerprint and no text of its own. So it says that a section changed, and never what changed in it. The author reads the section as it stands now, and a book kept under git shows there what the section said before.

=>

export const driftFaults = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  module: LoomModule,
  kept: ReadonlyArray<KeptRelation>,
): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> =>
  relationsOf(catalogue, module, kept)
    .filter((relation) => relation.status === 'drifted')
    .flatMap((relation) => faulty(DriftedReference({ name: relation.to }), relation.at).diagnostics)

## What changed, and what it left behind

A fault stands on each drifted reference, which suits an author who is reading the loom that holds it. An author who reviews asks the opposite question: which sections changed, and what did each leave behind. One changed section may have drifted many references, in many looms.

A `Drift` answers for one changed section. It names the section by its loom and by its title as the author wrote it, and it holds the relations that drifted from the section. `driftsIn` groups the drifted relations it is handed by the section they point to, and passes the others by. The relations may come from any number of looms, so a review of a folder groups across all of them. The groups come in the order their first relation was handed in.

=>

export type Drift = {
  readonly loom: Location
  readonly section: string
  readonly relations: ReadonlyArray<Relation>
}

const changedSection = (relation: Relation): string =>
  JSON.stringify([relation.target.module, sectionNameOf(relation.target.section)])

const titleOf = (section: LoomSection): string =>
  section.heading.title?.source ?? sectionNameOf(section)

export const driftsIn = (relations: ReadonlyArray<Relation>): ReadonlyArray<Drift> =>
  pipe(
    relations.filter((relation) => relation.status === 'drifted'),
    Array.groupBy(changedSection),
    Record.values,
    Array.map((drifted): Drift => {
      const { target } = Array.headNonEmpty(drifted)
      return { loom: target.module, section: titleOf(target.section), relations: drifted }
    }),
  )

## Approving

Approving a reference keeps the fingerprint of its section as the section stands now. The author has read the section and the reference still holds.

An approval covers the references its author chose and no others. The choice is a test on a relation. `chosenRelations` gives what to keep for each chosen relation of a loom: its labels as they stand, and the fingerprint its section has now, which `keptAs` puts together. Two references in one section that write the same name make one relation, so choosing either of them approves it.

`approvedRelations` gives everything to keep after an approval. That is what was chosen. Beside it stands every kept relation of this loom whose reference was found and not chosen, drifted or not, written with the labels its reference has now, so a relation that was followed through a rename takes its new names. A kept relation of the loom that found no reference does not stand, so what is kept holds the references that exist and no history. Every kept relation of another loom stays as it was. An untracked relation that was not chosen stays untracked.

What is kept comes sorted by its labels, so two authors who approve different looms change different lines of the file that keeps it.

=>

const byLabels: Order.Order<KeptRelation> = Order.mapInput(Order.String, keyOf)

const sameLabels = (one: Named, other: Named): boolean => keyOf(one) === keyOf(other)

const keptAs = (seen: Seen, fingerprint: string): KeptRelation => ({
  loom: seen.loom,
  section: seen.section,
  kind: seen.kind,
  to: seen.to,
  fingerprint,
})

const chosenIn = (
  tracked: ReadonlyArray<Tracked>,
  chosen: (relation: Relation) => boolean,
): ReadonlyArray<KeptRelation> =>
  Array.dedupeWith(
    tracked.filter((one) => chosen(relationOf(one))).map((one) => keptAs(one.seen, one.seen.now)),
    sameLabels,
  )

export const chosenRelations = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  module: LoomModule,
  kept: ReadonlyArray<KeptRelation>,
  chosen: (relation: Relation) => boolean,
): ReadonlyArray<KeptRelation> => chosenIn(trackedIn(catalogue, module, kept), chosen)

export const approvedRelations = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  module: LoomModule,
  kept: ReadonlyArray<KeptRelation>,
  chosen: (relation: Relation) => boolean,
): ReadonlyArray<KeptRelation> => {
  const tracked = trackedIn(catalogue, module, kept)
  const standing = Array.getSomes(
    tracked.map((one) => Option.map(one.kept, (held) => keptAs(one.seen, held.fingerprint))),
  )
  const found = new Set(Array.getSomes(tracked.map((one) => one.kept)))
  const elsewhere = kept.filter((held) => held.loom !== module.path && !found.has(held))
  return pipe(
    [...chosenIn(tracked, chosen), ...standing, ...elsewhere],
    Array.dedupeWith(sameLabels),
    Array.sort(byLabels),
  )
}

## One reference at a time, in the editor

In the editor an author approves one reference, the one under the cursor. `approvalsAt` gives an `Approval` for each relation of a loom whose reference meets a span of the loom's text and is not in step. A drifted relation is there to be approved again, and an untracked one to be tracked from now on.

An `Approval` holds the relation, and beside it everything the workspace keeps once that relation is approved, which `approvedRelations` gives. The editor writes it into the file that keeps relations, as the chapter "Loom's own diagnostics" describes. The compiler reads that file each time it judges a loom, so the reference is in step from the next judgement on.

=>

export type Approval = {
  readonly relation: Relation
  readonly keeps: ReadonlyArray<KeptRelation>
}

const meets = (at: Position, start: number, end: number): boolean =>
  at.start.offset <= end && start <= at.end.offset

export const approvalsAt = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  module: LoomModule,
  kept: ReadonlyArray<KeptRelation>,
  start: number,
  end: number,
): ReadonlyArray<Approval> =>
  relationsOf(catalogue, module, kept)
    .filter((relation) => relation.status !== 'inStep' && meets(relation.at, start, end))
    .map((relation) => ({
      relation,
      keeps: approvedRelations(catalogue, module, kept, (other) => sameLabels(other, relation)),
    }))

## What relations draw on

The corpus model gives the catalogue, a loom's module, the links of a section, the references its braces hold, and the three functions that resolve a name, an anchor, and such a reference to a section. The product pass gives a section's name, and the fault catalog gives `faulty` and the `DriftedReference` fault. Node's `createHash` computes the hash. Effect supplies `Schema` for the relation, and `Array`, `Option`, `Order`, `Record`, and `pipe` for the rest. `byType`, from the node foundation, tells the parts of a section's code apart.

=>

import { createHash } from 'node:crypto'
import { Array, Option, Order, pipe, Record, Schema } from 'effect'
import { byType } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import type { Diagnostic, Position } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import type { LoomSection } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomAst'
import type { Code, Part } from '@athrio/loom-ast/ProductAst'
import {
  type Catalogue,
  isTropeEntry,
  type Location,
  type LoomModule,
  referencesOf,
  resolveAnchor,
  resolveName,
  resolveReference,
  sectionLinksOf,
  type SectionRef,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'
import { DriftedReference, faulty } from '#ast/LoomFault'
import { sectionNameOf } from '#ast/ProductBuilder'

